#>design_whimsy
#:conventions #!style
#:layer #!pragmatics

^"thesis"{
  ~#note: "Whimsy is not decoration. It is earned delight through structural revelation and purposeful interaction."
  claim: "Spw's interaction design should reward exploration, familiarity, and sustained engagement. Whimsy achieves this by making the site feel alive, responsive, and delightfully aware of the user's presence."
  grounding: "Every whimsical element is grounded in the site's own semantics: operator physics, spirit cycle phases, brace topology, and valence qualities. Nothing is borrowed; everything is discovered from the work itself."
}

^"whimsy_principles"{
  "Calm at rest, delight on contact — the default state is quiet; interaction reveals personality."
  "Every interaction has micro-feedback — hovers, taps, and focuses get subtle responses that honor measurement, not just whim."
  "Timing is semantic — animations use the --duration-* ladder in tokens/core.css, and above --duration-base the rung is chosen by the scope of what changes. See @pacing."
  "Spirit phases are visible — frames and sections carry subtle visual hints of their semantic role through color and accent."
  "Symmetry is playful — operator pairs are recognized in interaction, not just documentation."
  "Exploration is rewarded — links get markers (#), buttons get glows, and hidden depth rewards sustained interaction."
  "Nothing traps — all animations are reversible; all interactions are discoverable; all states are recoverable."
  "No delight steals the hit target — spacing, focus rings, and touch affordances come before expressive motion."
  "A fundraising proof should feel cared for before it feels spectacular."
}

^"timing_vocabulary"{
  ~#note: "This block previously named practice_settle 100ms, threshold_reveal 300ms and full_express 600ms. None of those tokens has ever existed in public/ or scripts/ — see @alignment_audit#f2_timing_vocabulary. The real ladder is seven rungs, and it is owned by @pacing rather than restated here."

  owner: "@pacing — duration is one of four pacing terms and the least consequential. Do not answer a timing question here; answer it there."

  ladder: "--duration-instant 50 · snap 90 · fast 140 · base 220 · deliberate 360 · slow 480 · ceremonial 720, at tokens/core.css. All seven collapse to 1ms under html[data-spw-reduce-motion=on]."

  scope_law: "Above base, the rung follows the scope of what changes: a material property takes deliberate, a coupling between things takes slow, a whole frame takes ceremonial. Never choose a rung by how much the change matters."

  whimsy_default: "Whimsy is local by definition — one element responding to one reader. That is fast or base. A whimsical effect reaching for slow or ceremonial has mistaken delight for scope."
}

^"spirit_phase_visual_mapping"{
  ~#note: "This block previously assigned each spirit-cycle phase a literal HSL. That contradicted @coloring_depth, which requires colour to route through operator accent families rather than arbitrary hue — see @alignment_audit#f3_hardcoded_phase_color."

  axis_correction: "@one_physics#phase_axes separates four vocabularies that had been sharing one name. The spirit cycle is the ARC axis — where a visit is in its story. Hue belongs to the MATERIAL STATE axis, which grammar/syntax.css already routes to --op-*-color. An arc position is not a material and should not carry a hue of its own."

  how_arc_reads_instead: "Through accent FORM, which is what the entries below were always really describing, and through the operator each phase resonates with. @spirit_cycle already names that resonance per phase; where a phase needs colour, it borrows the accent family of its resonant operator rather than inventing a hue."

  initiation: {
    feel: "entry, threshold, new"
    roles: ["hub", "orientation"]
    accent: "top accent bar (gradient)"
    resonant_operators: "layer, probe — see @spirit_cycle"
  }

  resistance: {
    feel: "friction, encounter, pressure"
    roles: ["control", "settings"]
    accent: "subtle pulse on interaction"
    resonant_operators: "pragma, normalize"
  }

  transformation: {
    feel: "pivot, rupture, becoming"
    roles: ["interactive", "dynamic"]
    accent: "corner glow on hover"
    resonant_operators: "merge, object"
  }

  expression: {
    feel: "manifestation, projection, coming-forth"
    roles: ["category", "schema", "plan"]
    accent: "golden corner accent on cards"
    resonant_operators: "surface, action"
  }

  return: {
    feel: "settling, integration, arrival"
    roles: ["reference", "projection", "home"]
    accent: "bottom settle line"
    resonant_operators: "baseline, reference"
  }

  ~#open: "The arc axis is written to no attribute anywhere — see @pacing#>wonder_pacing_3. Until it is, these are readings of the design rather than states of a visit."
}

^"operator_interaction_whimsy"{
  ~#note: "How operators are revealed and celebrated through interaction without being intrusive."

  breathing: {
    element: ".frame-sigil, [data-spw-operator]"
    animation: "operator-breath (scale and letter-spacing shift on hover)"
    message: "This operator is responsive; it acknowledges you"
    timing: "local — base; whimsy does not climb the ladder"
  }

  glowing_chip: {
    element: ".operator-chip"
    animation: "radial glow appears on hover, subtle lift"
    message: "This is an interactive operator reference"
    timing: "local — base"
  }

  link_marker: {
    element: "a[href^='#']"
    animation: "# symbol appears after link on hover"
    message: "This link targets a named frame"
    timing: "local — fast"
  }

  indicator_pulse: {
    element: ".header-op-indicator"
    animation: "pulses subtly when header is hovered"
    message: "The current operator is being highlighted"
    timing: "local — base; whimsy does not climb the ladder"
  }
}

^"micro_interactions"{
  plan_card_hover: {
    what: "Card gets subtle corner accent (golden triangle), thesis gets bolded, wonder section glows"
    why: "Cards are discoveries; hovering shows what's special about this plan"
    timing: "staggered — the stagger is latency, not duration; each step is fast. See @pacing#four_terms."
  }

  button_press: {
    what: "Button scales down to 0.98, ripple glow appears from click point"
    why: "Tactile feedback confirms the button was pressed"
    timing: "fast for scale, base for glow"
  }

  list_item_hover: {
    what: "Item shifts left by 0.5rem, text shifts to active color"
    why: "Lists are navigation; hovering shows readiness to select"
    timing: "local — fast"
  }

  input_check: {
    what: "Checkbox/radio scales up 1.3x briefly, then settles to 1"
    why: "Forms need tactile feedback; animation confirms the check happened"
    timing: "local — fast"
  }

  frame_heading_underline: {
    what: "Horizontal line after heading fades in stronger on frame hover"
    why: "Frames have structure; the underline reveals structure through interaction"
    timing: "local — base"
  }
}

^"easter_eggs_and_rewards"{
  ~#note: "Sustained interaction discovers delightful moments that do not disrupt calm defaults."

  operator_breathing: {
    trigger: "hover over operator sigil for >1 second"
    reward: "operator breathes (scale/letter-spacing animation)"
    message: "Operators are alive and aware"
  }

  frame_phase_hints: {
    trigger: "hover over any site-frame"
    reward: "subtle colored accent bar at top reveals the frame's semantic role"
    message: "This frame is a hub / control / expression / reference / etc."
  }

  plan_corner_glow: {
    trigger: "hover over a plan card"
    reward: "golden corner triangle appears, wonder section glows with soft light"
    message: "This plan is special; wonder is present"
  }

  hashtag_reveal: {
    trigger: "hover over internal links (#target)"
    reward: "# symbol appears after the link text"
    message: "Internal links target named frames"
  }

  footer_line_glow: {
    trigger: "hover over footer"
    reward: "the border line glows more brightly"
    message: "Even the boundaries respond to your presence"
  }
}

^"accessibility_and_restraint"{
  ~#note: "Whimsy respects accessibility and does not trap or exclude users."

  reduced_motion: {
    rule: "Under html[data-spw-reduce-motion=on] all seven --duration-* rungs collapse to 1ms at tokens/core.css:2177-2184. Note it is a site-owned attribute, not a bare media query — the runtime holds an opinion about this state."
    message: "Reduced motion is a register, not a degradation. Collapsing duration leaves latency, cadence and arc intact, so the pacing survives — see @pacing#reduced_motion_is_a_register."
    implementation: "html[data-spw-reduce-motion=\"on\"], seeded from prefers-reduced-motion by prepaint-state.js"
  }

  dark_mode: {
    rule: "Glows and accents are more subtle in light mode, more visible in dark mode"
    message: "Whimsy scales to context; dark mode users still get delight"
    implementation: "@media (prefers-color-scheme: dark)"
  }

  mobile_performance: {
    rule: "Complex animations are reduced on mobile; tap feedback replaces hover"
    message: "Mobile devices get equivalent delights without performance cost"
    implementation: "@media (max-width: 768px)"
  }

  keyboard_navigation: {
    rule: "All hover states work on focus; all hovers are also accessible via keyboard"
    message: "Keyboard users get the same whimsy as mouse users"
    implementation: ":focus matches :hover styles"
  }

  no_flashing: {
    rule: "No animation flashes faster than 3 times per second"
    message: "Whimsy is safe for photosensitive users"
    implementation: "All animations are ≥300ms or have ≥300ms gaps"
  }

  state_parity: {
    rule: "Hover, focus-visible, tap, pressed, selected, loading, and dismissed states must communicate equivalent meaning even when the visual treatment differs."
    message: "A visitor should be able to understand state by keyboard, touch, pointer, or reduced-motion path."
    implementation: "Pair :hover with :focus-visible and durable aria/data-spw state selectors; never make hover the only disclosure path."
  }

  spacing_first: {
    rule: "Interactive ornament must not reduce text measure, touch target size, or local gap stability."
    message: "The site can feel alive without making the reader fight for space."
    implementation: "Reserve dimensions for badges, handles, focus rings, and loading labels before animating them."
  }
}

^"css_organization"{
  ~#note: "Whimsy is expressed through the layered CSS architecture rather than a single decorative file."

  file: "Layered owners under public/css/handles, effects, ornament, components, and route surfaces"
  sections: [
    "Typography whimsy (operator sigils, breathing)",
    "Frame & panel whimsy (perspective shifts, heading accents)",
    "Card whimsy (corner accents, glow effects)",
    "Operator chip whimsy (lift, glow, spacing)",
    "Navigation whimsy (underline animations, indicators)",
    "Button & interactive whimsy (ripple, scale)",
    "Spec pills whimsy (gradient shine effect)",
    "Input & form whimsy (pulse on check)",
    "Spirit phase visual hints (top accent bars)",
    "Footer whimsy (glowing border, fade effects)",
    "Accessibility (prefers-reduced-motion, color-scheme)",
    "Mobile adjustments (reduced animations, bigger touch targets)"
  ]

  integration: "Imported through public/css/style.css in the layer that owns the behavior; route-local whimsy should not override shared accessibility or spacing contracts."
  naming_convention: "Classes are semantic (plan-card, operator-chip, frame-sigil) not decorative"
}

^"fundraising_restraint"{
  ~#note: "How delight should behave when a page may become a proof surface for support."
  rule: "Let polish demonstrate care through spacing, predictable states, and recovery paths before adding new visual effects."
  useful_delight = "A subtle focus ring, clear selected state, stable card lift, or well-timed status cue can do more fundraising work than a large animation because it proves maintainability."
  avoid = "Do not ship hover-only jokes, cramped chips, overlapping chrome, or layout-shifting badges as proof of personality."
  route_expectation = "A supporter should be able to move from the featured surface to neighboring accessible routes and still feel the same hand in the spacing and states."
}

^"future_whimsy_opportunities"{
  ~#note: "Whimsy that could be added without breaking calm defaults. Two former entries were struck — see ^\"retired\" below."

  interaction_physics: "Charged braces (from brace physics) have visual glow that dissipates on interaction"
  discovery_trails: "Visited links leave subtle traces; the trail becomes visible through interaction"
  operator_color_shift: "Operator colors shift slightly based on time of day or phase of moon"
  collaborative_marks: "Multiple users on same page leaves subtle traces (future feature)"
  seasonal_ornaments: "Whimsy shifts with the seasons; motifs evolve through the year"
  responsive_whimsy: "Animations and effects change based on network speed and device capability"
}

^"retired"{
  ~#note: "Struck from the backlog because the site's own doctrine disqualifies them. Kept visible rather than deleted, so the reasoning survives the next time someone has the same good idea. See @alignment_audit#f4_disqualified_futures."

  constellation_animation: {
    was: "Home constellation renders itself slowly on page load"
    fails: "@wonder_doctrine#ambient_decoration — marker_1, present on page load without any interaction; marker_2, does not change with liminality or circuit level."
    also: "@arrival_electrostatics: do not author an entrance. Author a wall, and let the entrance be what crossing it looks like."
    salvage: "The constellation is worth revealing. Make the reveal something a reader crosses to, not something that happens at them."
  }

  parallax_depth: {
    was: "Subtle parallax on plan cards or frames based on scroll position"
    fails: "Same two markers — always present, unresponsive to engagement depth. @wonder_doctrine#ambient_decoration's inversion test asks whether an effect is more present at entry liminality than at deep; scroll parallax is equally present at both, which is backwards."
    salvage: "Depth that responds to liminality rather than to scroll offset would pass. That is @expressive_registers lift reading --spw-depth, which already exists."
  }
}

^"documentation_references"{
  @pacing: ~"../philosophy/pacing.spw"
  @one_physics: ~"../philosophy/one-physics.spw"
  @wonder_doctrine: ~"../philosophy/wonder-doctrine.spw"
  @spirit_cycle: ~"../philosophy/spirit-cycle.spw"
  @coloring_depth: ~"./coloring-depth.spw"
  @arrival_electrostatics: ~"./arrival-electrostatics.spw"
  @expressive_registers: ~"./expressive-registers.spw"
